          {
            title: 'Tarot',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'French Tarot is a trick-taking game for 3â€“5 players (usually 4) using a 78-card deck: 21 numbered trumps (atouts), the Excuse (Fool), and four suits of 14 cards (King, Queen, Knight, Jack, 10â€“1). Six cards form the Dog. Deal 18 each (4-player). Bidding: Petite, Garde, Garde Sans (without Dog), Garde Contre. Taker plays alone vs three defenders. Card points: bouts (21, 1, Excuse) and Kings 4.5 each; Queens 3.5; Knights 2.5; Jacks 1.5; others 0.5. Taker needs 56/51/41/36 points depending on number of Oudlers (bouts) in tricks.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: France. Tarot was introduced into France in the early 16th century; the dominant form now popular is the 19th-century rule set from Bourgogne-Franche-Comté. The game is played in France and French-speaking Canada.\nTime Period: Early 16th century introduction; 19th-century revival with Tarot Nouveau\nHistorical Development: Evolved from Italian tarocchi; French-suited Tarot Nouveau supplanted Tarot de Marseille; Fédération Française de Tarot formed 1973; second-most popular card game in France after Belote.\nCultural Significance: Popular social and competitive game in France; also played in French-speaking Canada.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
